Why THIS pin (#165 item 5, D9):

The vocabulary source must pass the conformance gate the twelve E8.2a review
rounds distilled: accept `models: read` (a hand table dropping it was wrong
about GitHub's vocabulary — the scope is real even though no workflow in this
tree declares it today), reject `id-token: read` (a hand table accepting it
was wrong — upstream removed `read` from id-token in github/docs#34306,
2024-08), and carry `vulnerability-alerts` (added in github/docs#60783,
2026-04).

Probed and refused:
- SchemaStore github-workflow.json (retrieved 2026-08-14): types id-token as
  generic read|write|none and omits vulnerability-alerts — fails the gate in
  both directions.
- github/docs HEAD (3363b628, 2026-07-30): the GitHub Models retirement
  (github/docs#62387) removed `models` from this reusable, while GitHub's
  EVALUATOR still accepts `models: read` (the live matrix is the oracle and
  the staged model workflows depend on it) — fails the gate.

c64d84db6c is the last revision before the retirement edit: the only upstream
revision carrying all three anchors in one file. The pin is deliberately
GENEROUS: an over-broad vocabulary can admit a scope GitHub later retires
(harmless — the staleness guard checks what the tree USES), while an
under-broad one rejects legal workflows (the recorded failure mode). To
refresh: run the recorded command against a newer commit and keep the
conformance gate green — if upstream never again carries all three anchors,
re-litigate the gate in an issue, never by editing the vendored bytes.
tests/test_auditor_workflows.py extracts scopes and value enums from this
file at test time — no hand-typed table exists.
